Basic structure of the protocol:

RESET
  - No args
  - Response: "OK"


GET
  Status
  - "OK %{Standby|InProgress|Regulating|Error}"
  RemoteMode
  - "OK %{Local|Remote}"
  DeviceType
  - "OK %s"
  DeviceId
  - "OK %s"
  Version
  - "OK %s"

  PressureInterface
  - "OK %s"
  CurrentPressure
  - "OK %.1e"
  PressureTolerance
  - "OK %d"
  PressureTarget
  - "OK %.1e"

  CurrentValveVoltage
  - "OK %.2f"
  CurrentValvePercentage
  - "OK %.2f"
  ValveVoltageMax
  - "OK %.2f"

  Timer
  - "OK %{Off|On}"
  TimerTarget
  - "OK %d"
  CurrentTimer
  - "OK %d"
  
  Regulation
  - "OK %{On|Off}"
  RegulationTimeout
  - "OK %d"
  ValueKI
  - "OK %.1e"
  ValueKP
  - "OK %.1e"



SET
  RemoteMode %{Local|Remote}
  - "OK %{Local|Remote}"
  
  PressureTarget %e
  - "OK %.1e"
    Errors:
    - RemoteModeIsLocal
    - OutOfRange
  PressureTolerance %d
  - "OK %d"
    Errors:s
    - RemoteModeIsLocal
    - OutOfRange  
  
  ValveVoltageMax %f
  - "OK %.2f"
    Errors:
    - RemoteModeIsLocal
    - OutOfRange
  ValveVoltage %f
  - "OK %.2f"
    Errors:
    - RemoteModeIsLocal
    - OutOfRange  
  
  Timer %{Off|On}
  - "OK %{Off|On}
    Errors:
    - RemoteModeIsLocal
  TimerTarget %{d}
  - "OK %d"
    Errors:
    - RemoteModeIsLocal
    - TimerIsRunning

  Regulation {Off|On}
  - "OK %{Off|On}"
    Errors:
    - RemoteModeIsLocal
    - OutOfRange
  RegulationTimeout %d
  - "OK %d"
    Errors:
    - RemoteModeIsLocal
    - OutOfRange
  ValueKP %e
  - "OK %.1e"
    Errors:
    - RemoteModeIsLocal
    - OutOfRange
    - RegulationIsRunning
  ValueKI %e
  - "OK %.1e"
    Errors:
    - RemoteModeIsLocal
    - OutOfRange
    - RegulationIsRunning
  
Other considerations:
  
Error handling:
 
RemoteModeIsLocal
- Could use an interlock preventing any SET being written to output unless
  RemoteMode is Remote.
- Could catch the mismatch and parse this specific error in each protocol 
  definition. 
  - Possibility - have handler call a record which sets alarm status of all
    input fields to Invalid.

OutOfRange
- Could define range limits internally in the records' EGUL/EGUF. Will need 
careful testing of bounds.
- Could handle OutOfRange replies and parse the relevant limits to truncate the
input.

TimerIsRunning
- Would need interlock to disable SET for timer when Status is not in permitted
  state.
  Can we do this without a race condition? I don't think there's any way to 
  atomically check Status and set Timer in the same operation, so not sure can 
  guarantee this.
- Could handle replies - only effect of pressing Timer button would be to cause 
  it to become invalid, which is probably fine. 

RegulationIsRunning

BadCommand
NoParameter
BadParameter      - Should not need to handle these as caused by syntax errors -
BadRemoteMode       can be eliminated by proper design of the protocol and database.
BadOnOff

Synchronization:
 
 - Device uses UDP, so we cannot rule out receiving any 
error condition based on the internal state of the device (e.g. we can't be certain
another application won't set the device to local when we send any given SET message).
That is, we can't guarantee that enforcing atomicity in the database will result in 
atomicity in the operations done in the device, unless we can be sure nothing else
can talk to the device.
Need to consider how to cope with this.

I think for now we will not perform any error handling - instead we should make 
sure that anything reporting on the internal state fetches the latest value from
the GET record, not the value in the SET record - including the RBV of the SET record.
No way to resolve the underlying synchronisation problem as UDP - all we can do is 
poll for latest data regularly, check everything is OK, and make sure UI reflects 
any failure to modify a variable. 



